ntion for a problem and  he dramatized it through description and  which was what John Steinbeck could do  or jacob riis with his journalism and  photography in the 1890s so I think one  kind of method methodological previs is  the particular second is to pick a cause  or an issue and stick to it don't write  one or two articles and move on to the  next subject the things I've done that  I'm most proud of written about for at  least two years consecutively  continuously in 1969 I began  to write about the problem of lead  poisoning in slum children and must have  written 25 articles over a two-year  period when John Lindsay was mayor of  New York because I was writing to have  an effect any politician any bureaucracy  can laugh off one or two articles but  when they know there's going to be 30  and that eventually television is going  to pick it up they begin to become  responsive and eventually Lindsay as  mayor appropriated a lot of money to  screen slum children for lead poisoning  and to fix up apartments where poisoned  children were found and eventually the  Congress Ted Kennedy in the congressman  from Manhattan named bill Ryan drafted  and passed a bill creating federal  funding to combat lead poisoning but  unfortunately Richard Nixon impounded  the money which is one of the thousand  reasons I hit Richard Nixon to this day  and I was once speaking at Columbia  Journalism school and I was asked by a  student who is your role model as a  journalist is it is that I have stolen  who I had talked a lot about in my  remarks or is it Jacob Riis and try to  be facetious I said my role models a  journalist is Joe Frazier what I was  trying to say was determination  relentlessness repetition aggression  never stopping I mean very sure the  students I think understood the metaphor  I was trying to create but there is an  element of what Joe Frazier did that I  try to apply to journalism and being  relentless and the another long crusade  of relentless follow ups involved a  young man named Bobby McLaughlin who was  convicted of murder in Brooklyn and was  in the sixth year of his 25 to life  sentence  when his foster father in a homicide  detective came to see me with all the  evidence from the case and the phone  numbers of the jurors and transcripts I  spent about three months reading  everything I went to visit Ravi and  greenhaven prison and became convinced  he was innocent and then we spent you  know year and a half writing about it I  got TV journalists involved like John  Miller and I got dick schaap to do a  piece on a the ABC network and after two  years a judge found that Bobby  McLaughlin who was innocent had been  wrongfully convicted and was released  from prison but it takes a couple years  of this kind of constant relentless  angry follow-ups to move a system  particularly a system like the criminal  justice system that is reluctant to  admit error and you just have to shame  people and at the same time being  activists go on radio shows go on TV  shows be a lobbyist which is what Jacob  Reese would do in lincoln steffans would  do to be an agitator and an organizer as  well as a muckraker Sarah did you know  our Lowenstein and what was your  impression of our Lowenstein oh boy I  knew I Lowenstein very well there's a  lot in my memoir about him I had  probably as it as mixed feelings about  al as i had about anyone I've ever known  he was he did so much good in Africa in  Mississippi in organizing the campaign  to dislodge Lyndon Johnson from the  presidency and 68 he started in the  spring of 67 and went around the country  organizing little groups of people  of students and professors and teachers  and even conservative students to go  against Johnson and he made people feel  less alone in their own resistance to  the war but he also did some awful stuff  he read baited my closest friends in the  civil rights movement like Bob Moses we  didn't speak for years about what he did  there he attacked the people who are  running the Vietnam moratorium in 69  because he couldn't control it the way I  think he couldn't control what Moses was  doing in the grass roots of Mississippi  with people like like mrs. Hamer I think  al al was a liberal who kept putting  himself in revolutionary situations and  he stayed a liberal and each and he was  a little bit of a control freak  particularly in Mississippi but I think  and it so I had no good and bad  experiences with him but I think his  loan campaign to begin a movement to to  drive Lyndon Johnson out of office over  the Vietnam War was one of the heroic  things in American history  okay I'm trying to figure out how to ask  you a question about contemporary  politics and you're you're here  promoting a memoir which is about  history so let me try it this way you  and Paul de Brule Road this very  insightful book about the that came out  of the fiscal crisis in New York City  the the first one of several it was the  abuse of power republish as the  permanent government which was the title  we originally wanted I see and and and  as I remembered you were very strongly  regretful that what had been created in  New York the high standard of public  goods in that era the Nixon era and the  ER that we were living in was being  stripped away and we don't have free  tuition higher education anymore we  don't have all those things that once  New York was emblematic of and that you  talked about the process in which they  were lost yet today you're here I think  still committed to the view of how to  create a majority that we had in the  early 60s absolutely and given the  distance that we've come how remote that  is how hard it would be to imagine  getting back to that but not getting  back to it is something to advocate but  getting back to it is something that  worked and worked as a majority politics  they have any misgivings about being  miss your ad politically I misgivings  about where I'm at yeah but he doubts  about care about what whether that view  of how to create a majority that we had  in the early 60s is still the one that  we ought to be advocating I think that  what dr. King and Robert Kennedy were  trying to do in 68 is as I said before  my model of how to get to a majority  Mike Harrington used to say we'd have to  aim for the frontier of the possible and  to locate that frontier and then fight  for it I don't know anything anyway I  think we've had a  a lot of defeats since the election of  Richard Nixon and a lot of things have  gone in a different direction in New  York City but my views have become  probably a lot more nuanced and  complicated as you grow older but I will  i still think the the speeches at RFK  gave in 68 in and the speeches I saw a  king given 59 in 63 and 65 and Selma and  67 at Riverside Church about the Vietnam  War are the new constitution that's  where we should go back to what's my if  I had a political slogan it would be  back to the future  thank you thank you very much please and  now mr. Newfield will sign some books  the line will form over here on the left  here and if you would help help us all  have room to walk and line up you could  fold your chair and leaning against  something stationary thanks very much  for coming out today and hearing mr.  Newfield jack new field has been a  columnist for The Village Voice New York  Daily News and New York Post his books  include the education of Jack new field  only in America Robert Kennedy his  latest is somebody's got to tell it the  upbeat memoir of a working-class  journalist it's published by st.  Martin's Press online at st. Martin's  calm
 

